## stdlib/fs.zag — Phase 2.1 read_file smoke-test example.
##
## Reads a small known file and prints its byte count. The exact
## file is `examples/basics/hello.zag` — a tiny source file with
## predictable content (assert its size is stable across zag
## revisions so the smoke run's expected-output pin holds).
##
## Heap-ownership contract (Phase 2.1 milestone): the returned
## `[]u8` is `page_allocator`-owned. Phase 2.1 adds a `.free_expr`
## slice-overload so `defer free data` lands on the slice-bearing
## slot via `std.heap.page_allocator.free(data)` — closing the
## ~4 KiB per-run leak that earlier phases tolerated via the OS's
## process-exit reclaim.
##
## Phase 2.1 discriminator contract: the slice overload fires
## when the let-binding carries an explicit `: []u8` (or `: []const
## u8`, `: []T`, `: str`) annotation. Untyped bindings (e.g.
## legacy `let data = read_file(...)`) fall through to the
## pointer-loading overload and would double-free at runtime —
## always annotate `read_file` slots explicitly.

import std.fs.{read_file};

fun main() {
    let data: []u8 = read_file("examples/basics/hello.zag");
    defer free(data);
    print("read {data.len} bytes\n");
}
